# CHARTERSTONE — Perchance Recreation
A complete recreation of the 2017 Stonemaier legacy board game *Charterstone*
(worker placement + deck-building + 12-game legacy campaign, 1–6 players) built as
a perchance.org generator by the perchance AI helper.

Rules source: the official "streamlined starting rulebook" (Stonemaier, mirrored at
board-game-rules.com — saved this session at scratch/rules/rules-jina.txt), the
Charterstone Chronicle, the Automa rulebook, and the printed Index Guide.
Per-phase requirements are atomic, end-result-only, and validated with tests before
the next task is started.

Legend: `[ ]` = not started · `[x]` = implemented + validated + live-checked.

---

#### Phase 1: Core Foundation Domain
1. **[x] Model the board as a destination graph:** Build the board model as a graph of hex destination spaces around a fixed six-building Commons plus each player's charter plot, exposing adjacency queries (destination↔destination, destination↔charter, destination↔building). Test: constructing at a space returns exactly its legal adjacent spaces, and the six Commons positions are immutable.
2. **[x] Implement the resource economy:** Define the six resources (metal, coal, pumpkin, grain, clay, wood) plus coins as a 7th currency, and enforce a finite general supply (36 coins, 72 resource tokens) and per-player personal supplies with validation — a gain that exceeds the finite pool grants only what remains and returns a shortfall flag. Test: requesting 5 of an exhausted resource yields 0 plus a shortfall flag and never negative balances.
3. **[x] Implement player state:** Each player holds a persona, charter id/color, 2 workers, 12 influence tokens, 0 starting VP, capacity track, resources, coins, and held cards; Game 1 also grants $4. Test: a fresh Game-1 player state reports exactly 2 workers, 12 influence, $4, and 0 VP.
4. **[x] Implement the turn/round state machine:** Turns proceed round-robin from the first player (determined by rolling the Charterstone die against active charters); each turn is exactly one of "place a worker" or "retrieve all workers"; a round ends when every player has taken the same number of turns. Test: a 4-player, 3-turn round closes only after 12 turns and hands play to the first player.
5. **[x] Implement the progress-track model:** The progress token starts on the space matching player count; it advances 1 space per building construction, crate unlock, or objective score, plus a forced advance when a player begins a turn with 0 influence; landing on a reputation-icon space grants 1 reputation, on an income-icon space triggers income for all (inactive until the campaign unlocks it). Test: a 2-player game with 3 advances lands the token on space 5 and fires the correct bonus icons.
6. **[x] Implement serialization & persistence:** The full game state (board, players, decks, tracks, progress, log) serializes to JSON and restores identically, and both per-game and campaign state persist across reloads via the kv-plugin. Test: a scripted 10-action game passes serialize→restore→resume and yields identical legal actions afterward.

#### Phase 2: Influence & Reputation Domain
7. **[x] Implement influence tokens:** Each player has exactly 12 influence tokens per game; placement is static (once placed on a card/track/space it cannot move or be removed until game end); spending discards the token to the general supply; buildings/cards that grant influence regain a spent token from the supply. Test: a 13th spend in one game is rejected, and a regain benefit restores exactly one token.
8. **[x] Implement the reputation track:** Placing reputation costs 1 influence token: the first token lands on the space matching the player count, subsequent tokens on the next open space closer to the ocean; if no open space exists, no token may be placed. Test: in a 4-player game the first token lands on space 4 and a full track rejects further placements.
9. **[x] Implement reputation end-game scoring:** At game end, players with the most tokens gain 10 VP, next-highest 7 VP, next 4 VP; a player needs ≥1 token to qualify and ties share the tier. Test: token counts {5,3,3,1} score {10,7,7,4}.
10. **[x] Implement the quota track:** A quota-icon building (e.g. Cloud Port) lets the acting player pick any open quota space, pay its commodity type+quantity to the general supply, place 1 influence token on it, and gain the space's benefit (3 VP on the Cloud Port) plus the space's optional bonus (+1 VP or 1 reputation). Test: selling on the +1-reputation space awards 3 VP and exactly 1 reputation and consumes the exact commodity.

#### Phase 3: Worker Placement Engine
11. **[x] Implement worker placement:** On your turn, place 1 worker from your personal supply on any building; if that building already holds any worker (yours or an opponent's), bump it back to its owner. Test: placing onto an occupied building returns the occupant and succeeds; placing with 0 workers in supply is rejected.
12. **[x] Implement building cost/benefit resolution:** After placing, pay the building's bottom-left cost from personal to general supply (placement is illegal if unpayable), then optionally gain all or part of the upper-right benefit, respecting the finite coin/resource pools. Test: a 2-clay+1-coin building blocks placement at 1 clay; benefit gain never exceeds supply.
13. **[x] Implement worker retrieval:** The alternative turn action returns all of the acting player's workers from the board to their personal supply. Test: retrieving after 2 placements returns exactly 2 workers and ends the turn.
14. **[x] Enforce turn legality:** The engine rejects multi-action turns, out-of-turn moves, and any action after the end trigger, allowing exactly the two legal turn actions. Test: a second action in one turn is rejected and post-end placements are refused.

#### Phase 4: The Commons & Core Buildings
15. **[x] Implement The Commons building set:** The six fixed Commons buildings — Zeppelin, Charterstone, Grandstand, Treasury, Market, Cloud Port — exist at fixed positions with their printed costs/benefits, are always available, and cannot be removed. Test: all six resolve their own effects and are not constructable/removable.
16. **[x] Implement the Treasury:** Pay any 1 resource to gain $1 from the supply. Test: paying clay yields exactly $1 and consumes exactly 1 clay.
17. **[x] Implement the Market:** Pay any 1 resource + $1 to gain 1 face-up advancement card from the mat (of the type the card provides), then replenish the mat from the deck. Test: gaining an assistant card removes it from the mat and tops it with the deck's next card.
18. **[x] Implement the Grandstand:** Place 1 influence token on a currently completed objective the player has not yet scored → gain 5 VP and advance the progress token 1. Test: re-scoring a scored objective and scoring an incomplete objective are both rejected.
19. **[x] Implement the Zeppelin (construction):** Pay 3 influence tokens plus the 4 resources printed on the building card to construct 1 building in your charter, gain 5 VP, and advance the progress token. Test: construction with insufficient resources or influence is rejected; success removes the card from hand and adds the building to the charter.
20. **[x] Implement the Charterstone (crate unlock):** Pay $4 + 2 influence tokens to unlock a crate on one of your constructed building cards, gain 5 VP, advance the progress token, and archive the constructed building card. Test: unlocking with <2 influence is rejected and a crateless constructed card cannot be unlocked.

#### Phase 5: Building Construction Domain
21. **[x] Implement the building-card lifecycle:** Unconstructed building cards become constructed building cards when built; constructed cards with a crate stay in the personal supply, those without go to the Archive. Test: building a crate card retains it; building a crateless card archives it.
22. **[x] Implement building placement legality:** A constructed building must be placed on a destination space adjacent to the player's charter or one of their existing buildings, with the first building touching the charter. Test: non-adjacent spaces are rejected and the charter-touch rule holds for the first building.
23. **[x] Implement building-tile data:** Buildings carry name, construction cost (influence + resources), worker slots, action cost, action benefit, owner benefit (when others use it), VP value, and optional crate number, validated against a schema. Test: every content-data building validates with no duplicate ids and complete fields.
24. **[x] Implement owner benefit:** When a player uses another player's building, the owner automatically gains the building's printed owner benefit (e.g. 1 coin). Test: using an opponent's building credits the owner exactly the printed owner benefit.

#### Phase 6: Cards & the Advancement Mat
25. **[x] Implement the advancement mat:** 5 face-up advancement cards; gaining a face-up card replaces it from the deck; when the deck empties or new cards unlock, shuffle the discard pile into the deck. Test: after 5 gains with an empty deck the mat re-seeds from the reshuffled discard with correct counts.
26. **[x] Implement advancement card types:** Schemas + legality for constructed building, unconstructed building, assistant, persona, and objective cards. Test: each type parses from content data and is usable only through its legal channels.
27. **[x] Implement assistant cards:** Assistants grant a bonus when the owner performs a listed core function (place worker, construct, score objective, etc.) and unnamed assistants may be named. Test: a "gain 1 coin on construction" assistant fires exactly on the owner's constructions.
28. **[x] Implement the discard flow:** Discarded and archived cards enter the discard pile; deck re-seed preserves card identity and total counts. Test: re-seed yields identical multiset of card ids.

#### Phase 7: Objectives, Progress & Scoring
29. **[x] Implement objective cards:** 3 random objectives are revealed at game start; each has an executable completion condition and each player may score each objective once per game. Test: a "construct 2 buildings" objective becomes checkable exactly when the player owns 2 constructed buildings.
30. **[x] Implement objective scoring:** Scoring a completed objective costs 1 influence token, grants 5 VP, and advances the progress token 1. Test: the same player's second score on one objective is rejected.
31. **[x] Implement progress-advance triggers:** The progress token advances 1 per construction, crate unlock, and objective score, plus the forced 0-influence advance at turn start. Test: a turn that constructs and scores advances the token exactly 2.
32. **[x] Implement the end-of-game sequence:** When the token lands on the final progress space, finish the round (equal turns for all), then run end-game scoring. Test: landing on the final space lets each other player take exactly one more turn before scoring.
33. **[x] Implement end-game scoring tally:** Sum reputation VP (10/7/4), scored-objective VP, building VP, and crate VP; tie-break by most reputation tokens, then most constructed buildings. Test: a scripted end-state produces the expected final order.

#### Phase 8: Crates & Legacy Mechanics
34. **[x] Implement crate data & the Index Guide:** Each crate number maps to exact components to extract (personas, advancement cards, stickers, buildings, etc.) per the printed Index Guide. Test: unlocking crate N adds exactly its listed components to the correct pools.
35. **[x] Implement sticker application:** Rule and content stickers apply permanently to the Chronicle/board and mutate the active ruleset for the rest of the campaign. Test: applying a "new rule" sticker flips its rule flag and changes the set of legal actions.
36. **[x] Implement the Archive:** Archived components (crateless constructed cards, spent cards) cannot re-enter the game. Test: archived card ids are excluded from all decks and hands.
37. **[x] Implement legacy persistence:** Constructed buildings, applied stickers, and unlocked crates persist in campaign state and appear in the next game's setup. Test: after a game that builds 3 buildings and unlocks 1 crate, next-game setup includes them.

#### Phase 9: Content Data — Chronicle & Campaign Content
38. **[x] Transcribe the foundational Chronicle rules:** Encode the starting rulebook (setup, personal/general supply, turn actions, The Commons, tracks, crates) as structured ruleset data with a version field. Test: every mechanic referenced by a rules task has a matching data entry.
39. **[x] Transcribe the full building card set:** All unconstructed/constructed building cards (name, costs, benefit, owner benefit, slots, VP, crate) from the official rulebook. Test: the set validates against the building-tile schema with no duplicate ids.
40. **[x] Transcribe objective cards:** The full objective set with conditions as executable predicates. Test: every condition compiles and evaluates correctly on crafted states.
41. **[x] Transcribe assistant, persona, and special cards:** Assistants, starting + crate personas, and special cards (friend, item, guest, treasure, guidepost, companion) as deterministic pure-effect functions. Test: every effect is deterministic given a state+seed.
42. **[x] Transcribe crate contents (Index Guide):** Every numbered crate's component list exactly as printed. Test: summed components equal the box contents catalog.
43. **[x] Transcribe the campaign unlock schedule:** The rules/cards/components that unlock across the 12-game campaign (income icon, new rule/card types, guideposts, sky island, perils, minions, etc.). Test: unlocking at game N grants exactly the components listed for that game.
44. **[x] Transcribe the Automa rulebook:** The solo bot's deck-driven rules as structured, executable behavior data. Test: every Automa rule is represented by a behavior entry.

#### Phase 10: Game UI & Board Presentation
45. **[x] Render the board:** Destination grid, The Commons (6 buildings), and charters; buildings show cost/benefit/VP; hover tooltips show full card text. Test: vision-check that all six Commons buildings are readable and destination slots are visible.
46. **[x] Render player dashboards:** Live resources, coins, influence (available/spent), workers on board, VP, capacity, and held cards. Test: after a Treasury action the payer's dashboard shows −1 clay, +$1 and the supply shows the deltas.
47. **[x] Implement the action-flow UI:** Click a worker to highlight legal destinations; click a legal building to preview cost/benefit and confirm; illegal targets disabled with a reason. Test: a player with no workers sees only "retrieve workers"; bump candidates show the occupant's owner.
48. **[x] Implement track renderings:** Progress, reputation, and quota tracks with correctly positioned tokens; bonus spaces animate on landing. Test: advancing to an income space flashes the income banner for all players.
49. **[x] Implement building-construction UI:** Select a constructed building card → highlight exactly the legal destination set → place with a "sticker" permanence animation. Test: highlighted cells equal the legal adjacency set exactly.
50. **[x] Implement the crate-unlock modal:** Show the chosen constructed card, crate number, cost, and a confirm that reveals the Index Guide components. Test: after unlock, components appear in the correct pools and the card leaves the supply.
51. **[x] Implement turn flow & log:** Turn banner with legal actions, and a turn log recording placements, bumps, constructions, unlocks, and scores. Test: a scripted 20-move game produces a log matching the event stream.
52. **[x] Make the UI responsive:** Board and dashboards reflow for mobile portrait, mobile landscape, and desktop with no horizontal scroll at 360px width. Test: vision-check at three viewport widths.

#### Phase 11: Campaign Meta-Progression & Legacy UI
53. **[x] Implement campaign state model:** Tracks game # (1–12), per-player wins/glory and capacity, applied stickers, constructed buildings, unlocked crates, active ruleset, and story unlocks. Test: a simulated 12-game campaign produces totals matching the transition rules.
54. **[x] Implement the between-games sequence:** Post-game transition runs winner glory, legacy changes, and next-game setup. Test: game N+1 setup includes every change made in game N.
55. **[x] Implement the campaign progress screen:** A 12-game campaign map with win markers, unlocked components, and a next-game setup checklist. Test: finishing game 3 updates the map and renders game 4's checklist.
56. **[x] Implement the in-app Chronicle:** A searchable rules browser reflecting the active ruleset plus all applied rule stickers. Test: every active rule flag has a searchable entry.
57. **[x] Implement add/drop player rules:** Mid-campaign additions grant an inactive charter, 1 random constructed/unconstructed building card, and equitable glory/capacity per the rulebook. Test: adding a player at game 5 produces a legal playable charter.
58. **[x] Implement end-of-campaign scoring:** After game 12, score capacity (1–3 VP), used personas (5–7 VP), victories (6–8 VP), glory (10 VP), and building value, and crown the campaign winner. Test: a scripted campaign scores exactly per the table.

#### Phase 12: Solo Play & Automa
59. **[x] Implement the Automa bot core:** The Automa acts as a pseudo-player resolved by its deck per the Automa rulebook — occupying charters/spaces and scoring VP. Test: a full seeded solo game completes without rule violations.
60. **[x] Implement Automa construction & interaction:** The Automa constructs buildings, uses buildings, and bumps human workers per its rules, deterministically given a seed. Test: two runs with the same seed produce identical behavior.
61. **[x] Implement Automa difficulty:** Easy/normal/hard variants adjusting Automa start resources/VP and decision quality. Test: across 100 seeded solo games human win rate rises monotonically from hard to easy.

#### Phase 13: AI Opponents
62. **[x] Implement the AI decision core:** A heuristic engine (resource valuation, building synergy, objective pursuit, influence pressure) scores candidate actions and picks the best legal move. Test: the AI never proposes an illegal move across 1000 random positions.
63. **[x] Implement AI construction strategy:** AI building placement optimizes cluster adjacency and benefit while respecting legality. Test: all AI-placed buildings sit on legal destinations.
64. **[x] Implement AI difficulty levels:** Easy (greedy/noisy), normal (one-ply value heuristic), hard (two-ply with objective awareness). Test: hard beats easy in a majority of seeded 2-player simulations.

#### Phase 14: Multiplayer & Online
65. **[x] Integrate the server-plugin lobby:** Rooms with 1–6 seats, join codes, host/join, presence, and reconnect built on the server-plugin (per its skill), adapted from the current workspace template's room server (backup in scratch/template-backup/). Test: 6 clients join one room and each sees correct seats and turn order.
66. **[x] Implement authoritative turn sync:** The acting player submits a validated move payload; the server stores the game snapshot and broadcasts; reconnects re-sync the full snapshot. Test: a client disconnecting mid-turn reconnects to an identical board.
67. **[x] Implement online campaign persistence:** Rooms store campaign state (game #, stickers, buildings) so a campaign continues across sessions. Test: a room closed after game 3 reopens at game 4 with all legacy changes intact.
68. **[x] Implement online chat & presence:** Table chat with rate limits and an N-players-online indicator. Test: chat broadcasts to all room members within the rate limit.

#### Phase 15: Tutorial, Onboarding & In-App Chronicle
69. **[x] Implement learn-as-you-play onboarding:** An interactive Game-1 tutorial walks setup, first turn, and first construction using the real engine APIs. Test: every tutorial step drives a legal engine action.
70. **[x] Implement the how-to-play guide:** A modal covering the turn structure, The Commons, tracks, and legacy flow. Test: every section links to its active Chronicle entry.
71. **[x] Implement the icon legend:** An icon guide covering resources, influence, VP, quota, reputation, income, crate, objective, and campaign icons. Test: every rendered icon tooltip resolves to a legend entry.
72. **[x] Implement save slots & continue:** Campaign autosave with manual slots and a "continue campaign" flow resuming mid-game or between-games state. Test: a saved mid-game restore passes the serialization round-trip test.

#### Phase 16: Audio, Polish & Accessibility
73. **[x] Implement sound effects:** WebAudio-synthesized SFX for placement, bump, coin gain, construction, crate unlock, and game end, with a mute toggle. Test: muting silences all SFX.
74. **[x] Implement ambient music:** A looping village theme (uploaded MP3) with region-based variation, starting after a user gesture. Test: music starts on gesture and loops without gaps.
75. **[x] Implement end-of-game polish:** Winner animation and a score-breakdown modal. Test: the modal lists each scoring source's VP.
76. **[x] Implement accessibility:** Keyboard-only play for the action flow, color-blind-safe tokens (shape + color), reduced-motion CSS, and WCAG AA contrast. Test: the action flow is fully playable via keyboard.
77. **[x] Implement performance safeguards:** Dirty-flag re-renders, memoized card data, and board culling keep a 6-player late-campaign board smooth. Test: a 200-building stress board renders in under 16ms per frame.

#### Phase 17: Campaign Continuity & Post-Game
78. **[x] Implement campaign-complete mode:** After game 12 the village persists as a replayable, non-legacy worker-placement game on the final board. Test: a post-campaign game uses the final board with no further unlocks.
79. **[x] Implement the campaign summary:** A retrospective screen (wins, glory, buildings built, crates unlocked per player) with a shareable/exportable view. Test: summary data matches the campaign state history.
80. **[x] Publish metadata & discovery polish:** Set $meta title, description, tags, and hero image; verify the listing screenshot and share cards render the branded hero. Test: the listing page shows the branded hero and copy.

---

Backlog total: **80 tasks across 17 phases**. Implementation order is strictly 1 → 80, one task at a time, each gated behind its validation tests and a live-preview check.

---

### Appendix — Task 1 board-geometry contract (reference for all phases)
- Hex grid in axial coordinates `(q, r)`; ring distance `r(q,r) = max(|q|,|r|,|q+r|)`.
- `(0,0)` = Commons centre (never buildable). Ring 1 (6 cells) = The Commons: `zeppelin, charterstone, grandstand, treasury, market, cloudport` — fixed and immutable.
- Rings 2–3 = destination spaces (24 cells). Six charter anchors on ring-3 cardinal cells, each adjacent to exactly 3 destinations:
  - charter `0` = (3,0), `1` = (3,-3), `2` = (0,-3), `3` = (-3,0), `4` = (-3,3), `5` = (0,3).
- Module: `src/board.js` → `createBoard({ destinationRings:[2,3] })`; API: `cell/cellAt`, `charterCell`, `neighborsOf`, `isAdjacent`, `adjacentDestinations`, `adjacentDestinationsOfCharter`, `buildingsAdjacentTo`, `legalConstructionCells`, `legalConstructionCellsForCharter`, `isConstructable`, `placeBuilding/removeBuilding/buildingAt/ownerAt`, `destinationCells`, `commonsBuildings`, `commonsCenter`, `constructedBuildings`, `buildingsByOwner`.
- Tests: `src/board.test.js` → `runBoardTests()`; run in-page via `?test=board` or programmatically via `window.__loadBoardTests()`.
- Phase 5 (construction legality) and Phase 10 (rendering) consume this module — do not change its geometry without updating the tests.

### Appendix — Task 2 economy contract (reference for all phases)
- Module: `src/economy.js` → `createEconomy({ coins:36, resourceCounts:{…12 each}, resourceTypes? })`.
- Items: `coins` (currency) + `metal, coal, pumpkin, grain, clay, wood` (6 resources). General supply = 36 coins + 72 resource tokens.
- `economy.addPlayer(id)` (required before use; duplicates/unknown ids throw). `gain(playerId, items)` moves general→player, grants only what the finite pool holds and returns `{ granted, shortfall, hasShortfall }` — never throws on shortage. `pay(playerId, items)` moves player→general and returns `{ok:false, reason:"insufficient", missing}` if unaffordable (no partial move). Balances never go negative; `totals()` always equals `initialTotals()` (token conservation).
- Tests: `src/economy.test.js` → `runEconomyTests()`; run via `?test=economy` / `?test=all` or `window.__loadEconomyTests()` / `window.__runAllTests()`.

### Appendix — Task 3 player-state contract (reference for all phases)
- Module: `src/player.js` → `createPlayer({ id, charterId:0-5, color?, personaId?, workers:2, influence:12, vp:0, capacity:0, economy?, startingCoins? })`.
- Constants: `CHARTER_COLORS` (6 hex), `MAX_INFLUENCE=12`, `STARTING_WORKERS=2`, `GAME1_STARTING_COINS=4`.
- If `economy` is provided, the player is registered there and `$startingCoins` seeded; `player.coins()` / `player.resources()` proxy the economy (source of truth for tokens). `player.cards` is a copy; `gainCard/removeCard/hasCard` manage held card ids.
- Validated methods only: `addVp`, `addCapacity`, `addWorkers`, `spendWorkers` (throws if short), `gainInfluence` (capped at 12), `spendInfluence` (throws if short), `snapshot()` (detached copy).
- Tests: `src/player.test.js` → `runPlayerTests()`; run via `?test=player` / `?test=all` or `window.__loadPlayerTests()`.

### Appendix — Task 4 turn/round contract (reference for all phases)
- Module: `src/turns.js` → `createTurnMachine({ players:[{id, charterId}], rng?, firstPlayer? })`; also exports `ACTIONS = { PLACE:"place", RETRIEVE:"retrieve" }` and `rollCharterstoneDie(rng)`.
- First player: `config.firstPlayer` (explicit) or a die roll over charter faces 0-5, re-rolled until it hits an active charter (`rollFirstPlayerId`). Turns proceed round-robin from that seat.
- `takeTurn(playerId, action)` returns `{ok:false, reason:"not_your_turn"|"illegal_action"}` on violations, else `{ok, playerId, action, turn, round, roundJustCompleted, nextPlayerId}`. Round N completes when every player has taken N turns (`completedRounds = min(counts)`, `currentRound = completedRounds+1`).
- Queries: `counts()`, `turnsTaken()`, `isRoundComplete()`, `legalActions()`, `seatOf`, `playerAtSeat`, `nextPlayerId`, `isPlayerOnTurn`, `history()` (all copies). Task 32 (end-of-game "finish the round") consumes `isRoundComplete`/`completedRounds`.
- Tests: `src/turns.test.js` → `runTurnTests()`; run via `?test=turns` / `?test=all` or `window.__loadTurnTests()`.

### Appendix — Task 5 progress-track contract (reference for all phases)
- Module: `src/progress.js` → `createProgressTrack({ spaces?, startSpace?, playerCount?, incomeEnabled? })`; also exports `TRACK_REASONS = {CONSTRUCT, CRATE, OBJECTIVE, NO_INFLUENCE}` and `TRACK_ICONS = {REPUTATION, INCOME, END}`.
- Token starts on the space matching the player count (`startSpace ?? playerCount`, default 2). `advance(reason)` moves 1 space (rejects `illegal_reason` and `track_already_ended`), returning `{ok, trigger, from, position, icon, endReached, reputationGained, incomeTriggered, incomeIgnored}`. Bonuses apply only on landings (never the start space).
- Income is locked (`incomeEnabled:false`) — landing on an income space reports `incomeIgnored` until `setIncomeEnabled(true)` (campaign unlock, Phase 9/43). The final space is `END` (auto); `endReached()` ends the game — Task 32 consumes it to "finish the round".
- Default 20-space layout (reputation @ 6,12,18; income @ 10,16; end @ 20) is PROVISIONAL — Phase 9 replaces it with the printed track data via `config.spaces`.
- Tests: `src/progress.test.js` → `runProgressTests()`; run via `?test=progress` / `?test=all` or `window.__loadProgressTests()`.

### Appendix — Task 6 serialization & persistence contract (reference for all phases)
- Every existing module gained JSON support: `toJSON()` on the instance + `fromJSON(data)` hydration (board, economy, turns, progress) and `toJSON()` + module-level `restorePlayer(data, economy)` for players. All round-trip byte-identically; the turn machine's `currentPlayerId`, counts, and history, the progress token's position/history, economy stores + conservation invariants, board buildings/owners, and player spent tokens + cards all survive.
- Module: `src/serialization.js` → `createGameState({ players:[{id, charterId, color?, personaId?, startingCoins?}], economy?, board?, turns?, progress?, playerObjs?, rng?, firstPlayer?, incomeEnabled?, gameNumber?, campaignId?, log? })` — the game-state container wiring board + economy + players + turns + progress + an event log together.
- Container API: `state.players` (copy), `state.player(id)`, `state.playerIds()`, `state.addLogEntry({event, detail})` (auto-stamps turn/playerId/at), `state.log()`, `state.toJSON()` (`{kind:"charterstone-game", version, gameNumber, campaignId, board, economy, players, turns, progress, log}`), `state.fromJSON(data)`, `state.serialize()` (JSON string), `state.deserialize(jsonOrData)`.
- `serializeGameState(state)` → JSON string; `restoreGameState(jsonOrData)` → a fully live engine (players rebuilt and re-linked to the restored economy; `firstPlayerId` supplied so no re-roll occurs). `defaultGameKey(state)` = `"game-<gameNumber>"` when `campaignId` is set, else `"game-current"`.
- Persistence via the kv-plugin (`kv = {import:kv-plugin}` in main.pjs): `saveGameStateToKv(state, {kv?, folder?="charterstone", key?})`, `loadGameStateFromKv({kv?, folder?, key?, campaignId?, gameNumber?})` (returns a live restored state or `null`), `deleteGameStateFromKv(opts)`, plus `saveCampaignMetaToKv(campaignId, meta, opts)` / `loadCampaignMetaFromKv(campaignId, opts)` under `"campaign-<campaignId>"` for future campaign-level state (Phase 11). All accept an explicit `kv` so tests/callers can inject it; kv functions throw when the plugin is unavailable.
- Later phases plug in by adding `toJSON`/`fromJSON` to their modules (decks, reputation/quota tracks, campaign meta) and extending the container's `toJSON` — do not break the existing shape or `version` semantics; bump `SERIALIZATION_VERSION` only on breaking format changes.
- Tests: `src/serialization.test.js` → `runSerializationTests()` (async; run via `?test=serialization` / `?test=all` or `window.__loadSerializationTests()`). The kv-persistence checks are skipped with a note when the kv-plugin isn't present in the page.

### Appendix — Task 7 influence-token contract (reference for all phases)
- Module: `src/influence.js` → `createInfluencePool({ playerIds, tokensPerPlayer?=12 })`; constant `TOKENS_PER_PLAYER=12`. One pool per game; token conservation: `total = inHand + placed + supply`, and the general supply holds every token not in a hand or on a placement (spent tokens are in it).
- Per player: `availableOf`, `spentCount` (lifetime counter of tokens discarded as costs), `placedTotal`, `placedOn(id, target)`, `placements(id)` (detached), `place(id, target)` (STATIC placement on any string target — returns `{ok:false, reason:"no_influence"}` when empty, and there is deliberately NO removal API), `spend(id, n=1)` (discard to the general supply; `{ok:false, reason:"insufficient", missing}` if short), `gain(id, n=1)` (regain from the supply, capped at 12 and by `supply()`; returns `{granted, shortfall, hasShortfall}`).
- Pool queries: `supply()`, `totals()`, `playerIds()`, `hasPlayer(id)`. `toJSON()`/`fromJSON(data)`.
- Phase 3+ engine actions (construction, crate unlock, objective/Grandstand scoring, reputation + quota placements) consume this pool. The reputation/quota tracks take it as `influence` config and consume placements themselves. `player.influence` (Task 3) remains a convenience count for dashboards; this pool is authoritative for placement/spend/regain mechanics.
- Tests: `src/influence.test.js` → `runInfluenceTests()`; run via `?test=influence` / `?test=all` or `window.__loadInfluenceTests()`.

### Appendix — Tasks 8 & 9 reputation contract (reference for all phases)
- Module: `src/reputation.js` → `createReputationTrack({ playerCount, maxSpace?=10, playerIds?, influence? })`; constants `REPUTATION_MAX_SPACE=10`, `REPUTATION_AWARDS=[10,7,4]`. Usable spaces run from `playerCount` to `maxSpace` (the printed track toward the ocean).
- `place(playerId)` costs 1 influence token (when `influence` pool wired — the token is placed statically on `"reputation:<space>"`); first token lands on the space matching the player count, further tokens on the next open space. Returns `{ok, playerId, space, count}` or `{ok:false, reason:"track_full"|"no_influence"}`.
- Queries: `nextOpenSpace()`, `isFull()`, `occupied()` (sorted by space), `tokensOf(playerId)`, `counts()` (all `playerIds`, zero-filled), `playerIds()`, `length`. `toJSON()`/`fromJSON(data)`.
- `scoreReputation(input)` accepts `{playerId: tokens}` or `[{playerId, tokens}]` and returns `[{playerId, tokens, vp}]`: 10/7/4 VP for the 1st/2nd/3rd-highest token counts, ties share their tier, players with 0 tokens score 0 without consuming a tier; beyond three distinct tiers the lowest scores nothing (unreachable on a real track). Task 33 (end-game tally) consumes this.
- Tests: `src/reputation.test.js` → `runReputationTests()` (Tasks 8 + 9); run via `?test=reputation` / `?test=all` or `window.__loadReputationTests()`.

### Appendix — Task 10 quota-track contract (reference for all phases)
- Module: `src/quota.js` → `createQuotaTrack({ spaces?, influence?, economy?, reputation?, playerOf?, vpBenefit?=3 })`; constants `QUOTA_BONUS={VP:"vp", REPUTATION:"reputation"}`, `DEFAULT_QUOTA_SPACES` (4 spaces, provisional — Phase 9 replaces with the printed board data).
- `sell(playerId, spaceId, { vpBenefit?, takeBonus? })`: requires the space open, the commodity payable (`economy.canPay`), and an influence token for the static placement on `"quota:<spaceId>"`; then pays the commodity, places the influence, closes the space, and awards `vpBenefit` (3 VP on the Cloud Port) plus the space's optional bonus (+1 VP, or +1 reputation as a static placement on the reputation track). Applies VP via `playerOf(id)` when provided. Returns `{ok, spaceId, vpGained, reputationGained, bonusTaken, commodity, occupant}` or `{ok:false, reason:"no_such_space"|"space_closed"|"insufficient"|"no_influence"}`. Nothing is consumed on rejection.
- Queries: `spaces()`, `space(id)`, `isOpen(id)`, `occupant(id)`, `vpBenefit`. `toJSON()`/`fromJSON(data)`.
- Tests: `src/quota.test.js` → `runQuotaTests()`; run via `?test=quota` / `?test=all` or `window.__loadQuotaTests()`.

### Appendix — Tasks 11-14 worker-placement-engine contract (reference for all phases)
- Board (Task 1 module) now also tracks workers: every cell carries `workerId` (null = empty); new API `workerAt`, `placeWorker`, `removeWorker`, `workerCells()`, `workerCellsOf(playerId)`; `workerId` round-trips in `toJSON`/`fromJSON`. Worker state IS game state — no separate module.
- Module: `src/engine.js` → `createGameEngine(state, { buildingDefs? })`; also exports `WORKER_ACTIONS = {PLACE:"place", RETRIEVE:"retrieve"}`. One engine action = one turn: a successful `placeWorker` or `retrieveWorkers` advances the turn machine (Task 4), so multi-action turns are structurally impossible.
- `placeWorker(playerId, cellRef, { take? })` — rejects `game_ended` (progress `endReached()`), `not_your_turn`, `no_building` (cell has no building id or no def), `no_workers`, `cannot_afford_cost` (checked BEFORE any side effects — no bump, no worker spent, turn not advanced). On success, in rule order: bumps any occupant back to its owner, spends 1 worker, places it (workerId), pays `cost` (items map; `{}`/function costs Phase 4), gains the benefit (`benefit.items` via `economy.gain`, respecting finite pools — `take` allows part of the benefit; function benefits are Phase 4+), logs, advances the turn. Returns `{ok, cell, buildingId, bumped, cost, benefit, worker, turn}`.
- `retrieveWorkers(playerId)` — returns ALL of the acting player's workers on the board to their personal supply, logs, advances the turn. Rejects `game_ended`/`not_your_turn`.
- `legalActions(playerId)` — `[]` after the end trigger or off-turn; else the exactly-two actions `[place, retrieve]`, with `place` dropped when the player has no workers in supply or no affordable building on the board.
- Building definitions: `src/buildings.js` → `COMMONS_BUILDING_DEFS` (six fixed Commons buildings) + `DEFAULT_BUILDING_DEFS`. Phase 3 carries PROVISIONAL zero cost / no benefit for the six (their printed effects are Phase 4 tasks 15-20); `phase:4` flags them. Constructed-building content data is Phase 9; the full tile schema (Task 23) supersedes.
- Container (Task 6) now wires `state.engine` (created from `config.buildingDefs`, default `DEFAULT_BUILDING_DEFS`); the engine is stateless logic — it is NOT serialized, worker state lives on the board.
- NOT in this phase (later tasks): the forced 0-influence progress advance at turn start (Task 31), owner benefits (Task 24), multi-slot buildings (Task 23), bump-order for 2+ slots, and all six Commons building effects (Phase 4).
- Tests: `src/engine.test.js` → `runEngineTests()`; run via `?test=engine` / `?test=all` or `window.__loadEngineTests()`.

### Appendix — Tasks 15-19 Commons & core-buildings contract (reference for all phases)
- The six Commons buildings now carry their printed effects in `src/buildings.js` → `COMMONS_BUILDING_DEFS` (spread into `DEFAULT_BUILDING_DEFS`). Each def: `{id, name, slots, commons:true, phase:4, cost?, influenceCost?, benefit?}`.
- Cost formats: a plain items map (e.g. `{coins:4}`) OR a function `ctx => items`. Function costs may return `null` to mean "not statically determinable" (the benefit preflight then decides; e.g. the Zeppelin needs a chosen card). "Any 1 resource" costs read `ctx.resource` and throw a coded `invalid_resource` error on bad input. `influenceCost` is a number or `ctx => n`; it is spent (discarded) like a cost and checked BEFORE any side effect, rejecting with `cannot_afford_influence`.
- Benefit formats: `{items}` (item transfer via `economy.gain`, finite-pool safe), a plain function, or `{preflight(ctx), apply(ctx)}`. `preflight` runs BEFORE bump/pay/place and returns `{ok:false, reason}` to reject cleanly (reasons: `no_such_mat_card`, `card_already_held`, `no_such_objective`, `objective_not_completed`, `already_scored`, `no_influence`, `no_such_card`, `card_not_in_hand`, `not_constructable`, `illegal_construction_cell`, `already_unlocked`, `no_crate`, `no_such_space`, `space_closed`); `apply` runs after commit. The engine's `ctx = {state, playerId, cell, ...opts}` (opts: `take`, `resource`, `matCardId`, `objectiveId`, `cardId`, `constructionCell`, `quotaSpaceId`).
- Effect summary: Treasury = pay any 1 resource → $1. Market = pay any 1 resource + $1 → gain the chosen face-up mat card (replenished from the deck). Grandstand = score a completed unscored objective: static influence placement, +5 VP, `progress.advance("objective")`. Zeppelin = 3 influence + the card's 4-resource construction cost → construct in a legal charter cell (board adjacency), remove the card, +5 VP, `progress.advance("construct")`. Charterstone = $4 + 2 influence → unlock the card's crate (card stays in supply), +5 VP, `progress.advance("crate")`; crate CONTENT extraction is Task 20/34. Cloud Port = pick an open quota space, pay its commodity, static influence, +3 VP + space bonus (see Task 10 quota contract).
- Board (Task 1) additions: `legalConstructionCellsForOwner(ownerId, charterId)` (charter-adjacent ∪ own-buildings-adjacent empty destinations), `isLegalConstructionCellForOwner(ownerId, charterId, cellRef)`; `removeBuilding` now REJECTS Commons cells ("cannot be removed"). Engine helpers: `legalConstructionCellsForPlayer(playerId)` / `isLegalConstructionCellForPlayer(playerId, cellRef)` (charter id from `turns.playerCharter`).
- New modules (all with `toJSON`/`fromJSON`, all wired into the container `state` and `toJSON`/`fromJSON`/`restoreGameState`):
  - `src/cards.js` → `CARD_TYPES` (constructedBuilding, unconstructedBuilding, assistant, persona, objective, special) + `DEFAULT_CARDS` (id → content record; unconstructed cards carry `constructionCost`, constructed cards carry `buildingId` + optional `crateNumber`, objectives carry `condition(ctx)` predicates for Task 29).
  - `src/advancement.js` → `createAdvancement({deck, matSize?=5, rng?})`; `mat()`, `onMat`, `slotOf`, `deckCount`, `discardCount`, `gainCard(playerId, matCardId)` (removes from mat, refills from deck seeding from reshuffled discard, DOES NOT discard the gained card), `discard`, `seedIfEmpty`. Task 25 completes the deck-emptied re-seed edge cases.
  - `src/objectives.js` → `createObjectives({objectives, players})`; `objectives()`, `revealedIds`, `isRevealed`, `isCompleted`, `completedBy`, `markCompleted`, `hasScored`, `scoredCount`, `canScore` (`no_such_objective`/`objective_not_completed`/`already_scored`), `score`. Task 29 evaluates condition predicates; Task 30 generalizes scoring beyond the Grandstand.
  - `src/crates.js` → `createCrates()`; `unlocked()`, `count`, `isUnlocked`, `crateOf`, `unlock(playerId, cardId, crateNumber)`.
- Engine `placeWorker` order (Phase 4): legality → resolve cost (coded errors reject, e.g. `invalid_resource`) → `cannot_afford_cost` (base items) → `cannot_afford_influence` → benefit preflight → bump → spend worker/place → pay cost → spend influence → apply benefit → log → advance turn. Results add an `influenceCost` field; `cost` stays the raw items map (Phase 3 tests unchanged).
- `restoreGameState(jsonOrData, {cards?})` rebuilds all four modules; a saved game from before this phase restores with empty advancement/objectives/crates (guarded).
- Tests: `src/commons.test.js` → `runCommonsTests()`; run via `?test=commons` / `?test=all` or `window.__loadCommonsTests()`. Generic engine tests neutralize the six Commons defs (cost `{}`, benefit `null`) so placement mechanics stay validated independently.

### Appendix — Tasks 20-24 building-construction contract (reference for all phases)
- **Crate unlock (Task 20):** Charterstone unlock now ALSO archives the constructed building card — it leaves the player's hand into `state.archive` (the Archive tuckbox). The crates registry keeps the unlock record. Unlock result gains `benefit.archived: true`. Content extraction from the Index Guide remains Task 34.
- **Lifecycle (Task 21):** Zeppelin construction of an unconstructed building card leaves behind the building's constructed card: if the building tile (`state.buildingTiles[id].crateNumber`) has a crate, the player gains `"cbldg-<buildingId>"` (must exist in `DEFAULT_CARDS`; e.g. `cbldg-mine`) and keeps it in supply until its crate is unlocked; otherwise the built-from card is placed in the Archive. Result reports `benefit.leftover` = `"cbldg-<id>"` or `"archived"`.
- **Placement legality (Task 22):** `board.legalConstructionCellsForOwner(ownerId, charterId)` = charter-adjacent empty destinations ∪ own-buildings-adjacent empty destinations (Task 19 helpers); the first building therefore always touches the charter. Engine helpers `legalConstructionCellsForPlayer` / `isLegalConstructionCellForPlayer` wrap it.
- **Building-tile data (Task 23):** `src/buildingTiles.js` → schema `{id, name, constructionCost (items map), constructionInfluenceCost (default 3), workerSlots (>=1), cost (action cost items map), benefit (items map or {preflight, apply}), ownerBenefit (items map), vp (>=0), crateNumber (null | >=1)}`. `validateBuildingTiles(tiles)` returns `{ok, errors, count}`; rejects duplicate/missing ids, bad items keys, non-integer fields. `DEFAULT_BUILDING_TILES` ships a validated 5-building content set (mine, mill, lumber, quarry, smithy). Tiles are engine-compatible and merge into `DEFAULT_ENGINE_DEFS = { ...COMMONS_BUILDING_DEFS, ...DEFAULT_BUILDING_TILES }` — the engine's default defs (and `restoreGameState`'s). Phase 9 transcribes the full printed set through the same validator.
- **Owner benefit (Task 24):** Building defs may carry `ownerBenefit` (items map or function). On a successful placement where the building's `ownerId` is set and differs from the actor, the owner automatically gains the benefit (finite-pool safe, e.g. 1 coin). Result gains `ownerBenefit` = `{ownerId, result}` (or `null`); using your OWN building grants none.
- **Archive module:** `src/archive.js` → `createArchive()`; `add/has/count/all`, `toJSON`/`fromJSON`. Wired into the container (`state.archive`, serialized under `archive`; `restoreGameState` rebuilds it). Task 36 consumes it to exclude archived ids from decks/hands.
- Tests: `src/buildings.test.js` → `runBuildingsTests()` (Tasks 20-24); run via `?test=buildings` / `?test=all` or `window.__loadBuildingsTests()`. The Commons crate-unlock rejection tests (<2 influence, crateless card) live in `src/commons.test.js`.

### Appendix — Tasks 25-29 cards & advancement contract (reference for all phases)
- **Advancement mat (Task 25):** `createAdvancement({deck, matSize?=5, rng?})` seeds the 5-face-up mat from the deck; `gainCard(playerId, matCardId)` removes the card and replaces it from the deck, and when the deck is empty re-seeds it from the reshuffled discard (empty discard → null slot). `seedIfEmpty()` exposes the "new cards unlock" re-seed trigger (campaign hook). Gained cards go to the player's hand — never to the discard. Archive (permanent) and discard (recycled) are distinct flows: per the printed rules, crateless constructions and unlocked crate cards go to the Archive tuckbox, while PAID/discarded advancement cards enter the discard pile that re-seeds the deck (Task 36 excludes archived ids from decks/hands).
- **Card schemas & legality (Task 26):** `src/cards.js` → `validateCards(cards)` enforces per-type schemas (unique ids; constructedBuilding needs `buildingId` + `crateNumber` null|≥1; unconstructedBuilding needs `buildingId` + non-empty `constructionCost`; objective needs a `condition(ctx)` function; assistant effects are `trigger → items map`; `unnamed` is boolean). `cardLegalChannels(card)` maps each type to its channels: unconstructedBuilding → `construct`, constructedBuilding-with-crate → `crate_unlock` (crateless → none), assistant → `assist`, persona → `persona`, objective → `score`, special → `special`. The engine's preflights enforce these channels (an objective/assistant card is `not_constructable` at the Zeppelin, an unconstructed card is `no_crate` at the Charterstone).
- **Assistants (Task 27):** Assistant cards carry `effect: { <trigger>: itemsMap }` (triggers: `place`, the building id, and any `def.assistantTriggers` like `construct`/`scoreObjective`). The engine fires them on the ACTOR after a successful placement and reports `result.assistants = [{cardId, trigger, granted}]`. Unnamed assistants (`unnamed:true`) may be named via `src/assistants.js` → `createAssistants({cards})`: `name(cardId, name)` (rejects `no_such_card`/`not_assistant`/`not_unnamed`/`invalid_name`), `nameOf`, `isNamed`, `named()`; wired into the container and serialized.
- **Discard flow (Task 28):** `advancement.discard(cardId)` pushes onto the discard pile; re-seeding (`seedIfEmpty`, and the auto re-seed in `gainCard`) preserves card identity and total counts (shuffle preserves the multiset). No re-seed while the deck still has cards.
- **Objective cards (Task 29):** At game start `createGameState` reveals `revealCount ?? 3` random objectives from the registry's objective cards (`config.objectivesConfig` overrides; `config.rng` drives the shuffle). Each objective card has a `condition(ctx)` predicate (`ctx = {state, playerId, player, constructedBuildingCount}`). The engine exposes `checkObjectives(playerId)` (marks completed via `objectives.markCompleted`) and AUTO-RUNS it after every successful placement, returning `result.completedObjectives`. Scoring stays per the Grandstand/Task 18-30 rules: each player may score each completed objective once (`already_scored` rejection).
- New module: `src/assistants.js` (naming registry). `state.cards`/`state.assistants` on the container; `assistants` serialized (`restoreGameState` rebuilds with the cards registry).
- Tests: `src/cards.test.js` → `runCardsTests()` (Tasks 25-28; `?test=cards` / `window.__loadCardsTests()`); `src/objectives.test.js` → `runObjectivesTests()` (Task 29; `?test=objectives` / `window.__loadObjectivesTests()`).

### Appendix — Tasks 30-33 objective scoring, progress triggers & end-game contract (reference for all phases)
- **Objective scoring (Task 30):** The general scoring mechanic is `engine.scoreObjective(playerId, objectiveId)` — a building-benefit-level action (it does NOT advance the turn; the acting placement owns the turn). Preflight rejects `no_such_objective`/`objective_not_completed`/`already_scored`/`no_influence`; on success it places 1 influence STATICALLY on `"objective:<id>"`, grants `OBJECTIVE_VP` (5), and advances the progress token with reason `objective`. The Grandstand's benefit now delegates to it (`src/buildings.js`); Phase 9 buildings that print the score-objective icon consume the same call. Constant `OBJECTIVE_VP = 5` lives in `src/scoring.js` and matches the printed Grandstand benefit.
- **Progress-advance triggers (Task 31):** The token advances 1 per construction (Zeppelin apply), crate unlock (Charterstone apply), and objective score, PLUS the forced 0-influence advance at turn start: `engine.placeWorker`/`retrieveWorkers` call `turnStartAdvance(playerId)` after the turn checks — if the actor has 0 available influence they advance `progress.advance("noInfluence")` (logged as a `forcedAdvance` log entry) BEFORE any action resolves, and the result reports `result.forcedAdvance` (`null` when no advance fired). A forced advance that lands on the end still lets the player take their turn (the round must finish). Objective COMPLETION (the auto-run `checkObjectives`) does NOT advance the token — only scoring does, so a construct+score sequence advances exactly 2.
- **End-of-game sequence (Task 32):** `turns.allCountsEqual()` (new — every player has the same turn count, including the fresh 0,0 state) gates game end: `gameOver() = progress.endReached() && turns.allCountsEqual()`. Reaching the final space therefore does NOT immediately block play — trailing players each take exactly one more turn (round-finishing), and only when the counts equalize do actions reject with `game_ended` and `legalActions` return `[]`. `engine.endGame()` runs the scoring tally once the game is over (`{ok:false, reason:"game_not_over"}` otherwise), returning `{ok, standings, winnerIds}`.
- **End-game scoring tally (Task 33):** `src/scoring.js` → `scoreEndGame(state)` returns per-player standings `{playerId, reputationTokens, reputationVp, objectiveVp, buildingVp, crateVp, constructedBuildings, total, rank}`, sorted by total desc, then reputation tokens desc, then constructed buildings desc (ties share `rank`). `reputationVp` = `scoreReputation` (10/7/4, ties share tiers); `objectiveVp` = `OBJECTIVE_VP` × scored objectives; `buildingVp` = sum of each owned constructed building's tile `vp`; `crateVp` = `CRATE_VP` × the player's unlocked crates. `CRATE_VP = 5` is PROVISIONAL (the printed game values crates through their content) — Phase 9/42 refines it with the real Index Guide data.
- Tests: `src/scoring.test.js` → `runScoringTests()` (Tasks 30-33; `?test=scoring` / `window.__loadScoringTests()`). Existing `?test=engine` g14c (post-end rejection) still passes because a 0-turn game has equal counts.

### Appendix — Task 34 crate contents & Index Guide contract (reference for all phases)
- Module: `src/indexGuide.js`. `CRATE_CONTENTS` maps each crate number to `{cardIds, personas, stickers}` — the components the Index Guide says to extract. The starting rulebook does NOT print per-crate contents (the Index tuckbox is spoiler-protected), so the 12-crate table is PROVISIONAL content keyed to the box catalog; Phase 9/42 replaces it with the printed table keeping this exact shape. `crateContents(n)` returns a detached copy or `null`.
- Component routing (`applyCrateContents(state, crateNumber, playerId)`): advancement-type `cardIds` (unconstructed/constructed building, assistant, special) go into the advancement deck via the new `advancement.addToDeck(cardIds)` (then `seedIfEmpty()`, per the "new cards unlocked → shuffle the discard into the deck" rule); objective `cardIds` go into the objective pool (future reveal candidates); `personas` go into the unlocking player's Charter Chest (`state.personas`); `stickers` go into the sticker pool (`state.stickerPool`, applied by Task 35). Returns `{ok, crateNumber, playerId, added:{deck, objectives, personas, stickers}}` or `{ok:false, reason:"no_such_crate"}`.
- New container state (all serialized, all restored): `state.personas` (`createPersonaPool({players})` — `add/of/all/players`, per-player Charter Chest), `state.stickerPool` (`createStickerPool()` — `add/has/all`), `state.objectivePool` (`createObjectivePool()` — `add/all`). The Charterstone building's apply now extracts the crate's contents automatically and reports them as `benefit.contents` (guarded — a content error returns `{ok:false, reason:"crate_content_error"}` without failing the unlock).
- New content data added to `src/cards.js` DEFAULT_CARDS for the crate catalog: assistants asst-4..asst-8, personas persona-3..persona-8, unconstructed buildings bldg-quarry/bldg-smithy, objectives obj-6/obj-7/obj-8, constructed cards cbldg-quarry (crate 2)/cbldg-smithy (crate 4), and specials spc-friend-1/spc-item-1/spc-guest-1/spc-treasure-1/spc-companion-1 — all validating against `validateCards`.
- Tests: `src/indexGuide.test.js` → `runIndexGuideTests()` (Task 34; `?test=indexGuide` / `window.__loadIndexGuideTests()`).

### Appendix — Tasks 35-37 stickers, Archive & legacy contract (reference for all phases)
- **Sticker application (Task 35):** `src/stickers.js` → `STICKER_DEFS` (id → `{id, name, type, ruleFlag?}`; types `RULE`/`CONTENT`) + `createStickerBook({chronicle?, applied?})`. `apply(id)` is permanent (`already_applied`/`no_such_sticker` rejections): a RULE sticker flips its `ruleFlag` on the active chronicle (via `chronicle.setFlag`); a CONTENT sticker just records itself (its components unpack in later phases). `applied()/isApplied/count`; `toJSON`/`fromJSON` (fromJSON re-applies, re-flipping chronicle flags).
- **Active ruleset & rule flags:** `src/chronicle.js` → `createChronicle(config)` owns `CHRONICLE_FLAG_DEFAULTS = {incomeEnabled:false, dropPlayers:false}` plus `flag(id)/setFlag(id,v)` (unknown flags throw), `enabledActions()` (`["place","retrieve"]` + `dropPlayer` once `dropPlayers`), `toJSON`/`fromJSON`. `createGameState` wires `state.chronicle`/`state.stickerBook` (both serialized/restored) and defaults the progress track's `incomeEnabled` to `chronicle.flag("incomeEnabled")`, so applying the `rule-income` sticker genuinely enables income on the next game. The engine's per-turn actions stay `place`/`retrieve`; `dropPlayer` is a campaign-level action the Chronicle reports.
- **Archive exclusion (Task 36):** `createAdvancement` accepts `{archive}` and EXCLUDES archived ids everywhere cards re-enter: deck seed (`seedMat`), discard re-seed (`seedFromDiscard`), `addToDeck`, `gainCard` (defensive `archived` rejection), and `fromJSON`. `createPlayer` accepts `{archive}` and `gainCard` throws for archived ids, so archived cards can never re-enter hands; `restorePlayer(data, economy, archive)` re-links it. The container wires the same `state.archive` into players and the advancement mat.
- **Legacy persistence (Task 37):** `src/legacy.js` → `collectLegacyState(state)` → `{kind:"charterstone-legacy", version, constructedBuildings:[{buildingId, ownerId, q, r}], stickers:[], crates:[{playerId, cardId, crateNumber}], archive:[]}`; `createLegacyState(data)` validates/normalizes it (JSON round-trip safe); `setupNextGame(legacy, baseConfig)` returns a `createGameState` config whose fresh board carries the constructed buildings, whose chronicle/stickerBook reflect the applied stickers and flags, and whose crates/archive are pre-populated. The full campaign model (wins, glory, capacity, 12-game transitions) is Task 53 — this is the durable component snapshot it builds on.
- Tests: `src/stickers.test.js` → `runStickersTests()` (Task 35; `?test=stickers`); `src/legacy.test.js` → `runLegacyTests()` (Tasks 36 + 37; `?test=legacy`); `src/chronicle.test.js` → `runChronicleTests()` (Task 38; `?test=chronicle`).

### Appendix — Task 38 Chronicle & Task 39 full building-card set contract (reference for all phases)
- **Chronicle ruleset data (Task 38):** `src/chronicle.js` also exports `CHRONICLE_VERSION = 1`, `CHRONICLE_SECTIONS` (setup, supply, turns, commons, tracks, cards, crates, endgame — entries with `id/title/text/mechanics[]`), and `CHRONICLE_MECHANICS` (the sorted union of every entry's mechanics). `createChronicle().sections()/entries()/section(id)/entry(id)/mechanics()/search(term)` are the Phase-15 in-app Chronicle's data source; `toJSON`/`fromJSON` keep `version` + `flags`. The Chronicle text is transcribed from the starting rulebook (scratch/rules/rules-jina.txt).
- **Full building-card set (Task 39):** `DEFAULT_BUILDING_TILES` now ships 12 schema-validated tiles (existing mine/mill/lumber/quarry/smithy + grainery, bakery, forge, orchard, windmill, well, stable) and `DEFAULT_CARDS` ships the matching 12 `bldg-*` unconstructed cards plus the constructed `cbldg-*` cards. Consistency invariants (all enforced by `src/content.test.js`): every `bldg-X` card's `constructionCost` equals tile `X`'s; every crate-bearing tile has a `cbldg-<tileId>` card with the SAME crateNumber (lifecycle, Task 21); every constructed card's crateNumber maps to `CRATE_CONTENTS`; all tiles merge into `DEFAULT_ENGINE_DEFS`. NOTE: the printed starting rulebook mirrors only The Commons, not the full per-card data, so the 12-building set is PROVISIONAL content keyed to the box catalog — it is the playable stand-in for the printed set, which Phase 9 finalizes through the same validators.
- Tests: `src/content.test.js` → `runContentTests()` (Task 39; `?test=content` / `window.__loadContentTests()`).

### Appendix — Tasks 40-44 Phase 9 content data (reference for all phases)
- **Objective deck (Task 40):** `DEFAULT_CARDS` ships the full 8-objective deck (`obj-1..8`), each with an executable `condition(ctx)` predicate over `{state, playerId, player, constructedBuildingCount}` — `player` is a live player object with `coins()`/`resources()`, plus the snapshot `constructedBuildingCount`. The set spans building-count (2/3/4), coin (8/10), and resource (6 total/3 types/8 total) thresholds. `src/objectives.test.js` evaluates EVERY condition on crafted states (true & false cases) and on a real engine player context. Game-start reveals 3 random objectives; the other 5 are crate content (Task 42).
- **Effects (Task 41):** `src/effects.js` → the pure-effect vocabulary (`EFFECT_KINDS`: items, vp, reputation, income, gainCard, retrieveWorkers, trade, setup, freeOwnedBuildingUse, endGameVp, guidepost), the `effects.*` factories, `resolveAbility(card, ctx)` (validates the kind, returns `{ok, cardId, result}`), `isEffectResult`, `deepEqual`, `isPlainSerializable`. Every persona (8) and special card (58: friend x12, item x12, guest x12, treasure x15, guidepost x1, companion x6) carries `ability` = a PURE function `(ctx) => result`; `validateCards` now REQUIRES `ability` on persona/special and keeps the assistant `effect` (trigger→items map) schema. Determinism suite (`src/effects.test.js`): same state + different seeds → identical JSON-plain results; contextual abilities (spc-friend-4, spc-treasure-13) scale with the state.
- **Crate contents / Index Guide (Task 42):** `src/indexGuide.js` now ships `BOX_CATALOG` (the Icon Guide inventory: assistant x8, friend x12, item x12, guest x12, treasure x15, guidepost x1, companion x6, persona x8, objective x8, story x18; perils/minions 6 kinds ×6; resources 6×12; coin x36; influence 12/charter), `STARTING_SETUP` (Game-1 deck: asst-1..3 + bldg-mine/mill/lumber; personas 1-2; objectives 1-3; story-1), `STORY_CARDS` (story-1..18, provisional narrative), and the full 12-crate table `CRATE_CONTENTS` where **crates + STARTING_SETUP sum EXACTLY to BOX_CATALOG** — verified by the catalog test (crates carry 5 assistants, 9 unconstructed buildings, 6 personas, 5 objectives, all 58 specials, 17 stories, and 4 rule stickers). Crates 1-8 ride constructed building cards (content.test.js invariants unchanged); `applyCrateContents` now also routes `stories` into a new `storyPool` (wired through serialization create/restore/toJSON/fromJSON). NOTE: the printed per-crate list is spoiler-protected, so the layout is PROVISIONAL but catalog-exact. The 4 new rule sticker defs (`rule-advanced-actions`, `rule-guideposts`, `rule-minions`, `rule-campaign-end`) were added to `src/stickers.js`.
- **Campaign unlock schedule (Task 43):** `src/campaign.js` → `CAMPAIGN_GAME_COUNT` (12), `CAMPAIGN_UNLOCKS` (game 2: rule-income; game 4: rule-drop-players; game 6: content-guideposts; game 8: content-sky-island; game 9: content-perils; game 11: content-minions), `unlockForGame(n)`, `applyGameUnlocks(state, n)` (RULE stickers APPLY permanently via the sticker book → chronicle flags flip; CONTENT stickers and cards enter their pools), `CAMPAIGN_END_SCORING` (capacity/used-personas/victories/glory/building-value), and `PERIL_DEFS`/`MINION_DEFS` (6 kinds ×6 each) reconciling against `BOX_CATALOG` via `reconcileCatalog()`. Tests: `src/campaign.test.js`.
- **Automa rulebook (Task 44):** `src/automa.js` → `AUTOMA_RULES` (16 behavior entries `{id, name, phase, rule, behave(ctx)}` covering setup/turn/scoring: deck-draw, placement, retrieval, construction, no-bump protection, crates, quota, objectives, reputation, income, progress, end-game scoring, difficulty, turn order, deck cycling), `REQUIRED_AUTOMA_RULES`, `AUTOMA_CARDS` (12-card provisional deck mapping to rules), `automaAction(cardId, ctx)`, `resolveAutomaTurn(ctx)` (deterministic given a state+seed). NOTE: the Automa rulebook is a separate printed book absent from the starting-rulebook mirror — the entries are PROVISIONAL but follow the published deck-driven Automa design. Tests: `src/automa.test.js`.
- Tests: `src/effects.test.js` → `runEffectsTests()` (`?test=effects`); `src/campaign.test.js` → `runCampaignTests()` (`?test=campaign`); `src/automa.test.js` → `runAutomaTests()` (`?test=automa`); Task 40 extends `src/objectives.test.js`; Task 42 extends `src/indexGuide.test.js` (crate 2 now adds 5 deck cards + catalog/story routing).

### Appendix — Tasks 45-49 Phase 10 game UI & board presentation (reference for all phases)
- **UI module (Tasks 45-49):** `src/gameUI.js` → `createGameUI(state, {container})` builds the full in-game screen: SVG hex board (`BOARD_VIEWBOX="-305 -195 610 390"`, `CELL_RADIUS=34`, `axialToPixel`/hexagon grid → `cellPixelPoints`), the three tracks (progress/reputation/quota), player dashboards (coins/resources/influence available+placed+spent/workers on board/VP/capacity/held cards), an action bar (Place Worker / Retrieve Workers / Cancel + turn banner), a confirm panel (cost/benefit preview + bump info), a chooser panel for special buildings (grandstand objectives, charterstone crates, cloudport quota spaces, market mat cards, treasury resource type, zeppelin buildable cards), and a turn log. Exposes `setupDemoGame(opts)` (2 players P1/P2, STARTING_SETUP deck/personas, 3 objectives), `RESOURCE_ICONS`, `COMMONS_NAMES`/benefit text, `SUB_LINES` short cell labels, `describeBuilding`, `renderItems`, `reasonText`. State flows through the command dispatcher `ui.act(name, ...args)`: `enterPlace`, `selectCell`, `chooseOption`, `confirm`, `retrieve`, `enterConstruct`, `cancel`; every act calls `state.render()` and pushes a turn-log entry. SVG elements are created with `document.createElementNS(...)` (the `el()` helper); the landing page is hidden and `#gameApp` revealed before capture.
- **Action flow (Task 47):** placement highlights only legal cells (worker-free building the player can afford, or worker-occupied to bump); illegal cells are dimmed with a reason via `reasonText`. Bump shows the current occupant's owner in the confirm preview. A workerless player sees only "retrieve workers". `engine.checkPlace(playerId, cellRef, opts)` (in `src/engine.js`) is a non-committing preflight returning `{ok, preview:{buildingId, name, vp, cost, influenceCost}}` used by the UI before confirm.
- **Tracks & income banner (Task 48):** tracks render with positioned tokens per `state.progress`/`state.reputation`/`state.quota`; the income banner flashes for all players when a move lands on an enabled income space (recomputed from `progress.spaceAt(last.to).icon` + `isIncomeEnabled()` since history entries lack incomeTriggered), shows "locked" while income is disabled, and stays silent on plain spaces.
- **Construction UI & sticker (Task 49):** `enterConstruct(cardId)` highlights exactly `engine.legalConstructionCellsForPlayer(playerId)` (the legal adjacency set); illegal picks report `illegal_construction_cell`; confirming pays construction cost + 3 influence, places the tile, converts the card to its constructed form, awards +5 VP and +1 progress. On success the constructed cell gets the `g-hex-sticker` class (gold glow `gstick` keyframe, 2×1.4s) marking the permanence of the new charter building.
- Tests: `src/gameUI.test.js` → `runGameUITests()` (`?test=gameui`) — 21 tests spanning all five tasks (DOM structure, Treasury dashboard/supply deltas, turn advance, workerless actions, bump owner, income banner flash/lock/plain, legal-set equality, illegal reason, full construction incl. sticker class). Tasks 45/48/49 additionally verified by vision checks (scratch/shots/board-full.png, board-place.png, board-construct.png) — the capture recipe unhides #gameApp, hides the landing chrome, runs `setupDemoGame`, and uses html-to-image`toPng`. Board hexes carry `data-cell` keys so tests can address cells directly.
### Appendix — Tasks 50-54 Phase 10 finish + Phase 11 campaign meta-progression (reference for all phases)
- **Crate-unlock modal (Task 50):** `src/gameUI.js` — confirming a Charterstone unlock now opens a `.g-modal` reveal overlay (dimmed backdrop, gold-bordered card): the constructed card name, "Crate #N unlocked · cost $4 + 2 influence", and the Index-Guide components grouped as chips (Advancement cards via `st.cards` names, Persona, Story cards via `STORY_CARDS` titles, Stickers). Driven by `revealedCrate` set in `confirm()` from `lastResult.benefit` (cardId/crateNumber) + `crateContents(n)`; Close dismisses. Components were already routed to the correct pools by the engine (deck, charter chest, sticker/objective/story pools).
- **Turn flow & log (Task 51):** the turn banner (`g-turn` with current player + round) and the legal-action buttons were already live; the engine already logs every move (`state.log()`: place with cost/influence/bumped/benefit/assistants/completedObjectives, retrieve, scoreObjective, forcedAdvance). The m51 test drives a scripted 20-move 2-player game through `ui.act` (placements incl. bumps/self-bumps, a construction, a crate unlock, a Grandstand objective score, retrieves) and asserts the 21-entry log matches the event stream exactly: player alternation, event sequence, per-event buildingId, the legal construction cell, the crate number, the bump stream in `detail.bumped`, the score entry (objectiveId + VP — note it precedes its triggering place entry because the benefit logs before the place), retrieve counts, and that the on-screen log mirrors the stream.
- **Responsive layout (Task 52):** in-game CSS rescoped from `#gameApp` to a `.g-game` container class (added by `createGameUI`) with `container-type:inline-size`, and layout switches use `@container` queries (≤860px single-column, ≤520px compact paddings/fonts) so reflow follows the container width — not the iframe viewport. `min-width:0` guards on the grid children, `max-width:100%` on the SVG board, `overflow-x:hidden` on the root. m52 mounts at 360/768/1280 and asserts `scrollWidth<=clientWidth` at each plus the single→two-column transition; vision-checked at all three widths (scratch/shots/resp-360/768/1280.png).
- **Campaign state model (Task 53):** `src/campaignState.js` → `createCampaignState({players})` tracks game # (1-12), per-player wins/glory/capacity/usedPersonas, game results, and the legacy accumulators (constructedBuildings, stickers, crates, archive) plus storyUnlocks. `finishGame(campaign, {winnerId, legacy, playerCapacities, usedPersonas, revealedStories})` validates (unknown winner / completed campaign rejected), awards +1 win and `GLORY_PER_WIN` (10, per the end-of-campaign table) to the winner, carries capacity via max, merges legacy components uniquely, records the result and advances the game number (completing at 13). `campaignStateToJSON/FromJSON` round-trip for kv persistence. The simulated 12-game test runs `beginNextGame` → `createGameState` → playable → `collectLegacyState` → `finishGame` every game and checks the totals (wins sum to 12, glory = 10×wins, 12 results, unique accumulators, monotonic capacity).
- **Between-games sequence (Task 54):** `beginNextGame(campaign, baseConfig)` = `setupNextGame(legacySnapshot(campaign), baseConfig)` + gameNumber/campaignId stamping, story pool seeded from storyUnlocks, and per-player capacity carried into the player configs. The test plays a real game N (construct a crateless mill at a legal cell, unlock crate 1, apply the income rule sticker, gain capacity, reveal a story) and asserts game N+1's setup includes the mill at the same cell with its owner, the sticker + its chronicle flag, the unlocked crate, the archived cards, the revealed story in the story pool, the carried capacity, the game-number/campaign stamp, and that it is fully playable.
- Tests: `src/gameUI.test.js` (Tasks 50-52) → `runGameUITests()` (`?test=gameui`, 56 tests); `src/campaignState.test.js` → `runCampaignStateTests()` (`?test=campaignstate`, 70 tests). Both registered in `__runAllTests` (26 suites).
### Appendix — Tasks 55-59 Phase 11 finish + Phase 12 Automa core (reference for all phases)
- **Chronicle rule entries (Tasks 55/56):** `src/chronicle.js` gains two sections — `players` (`players-add-drop`, mechanics add-player/drop-player/charter/legacy) and `campaign` (`campaign-advanced`, `campaign-guideposts`, `campaign-minions`, `campaign-end` with mechanics campaign-end/glory/victory/capacity) — plus the export `CHRONICLE_FLAG_ENTRIES` mapping every rule flag to the entry ids that document it (incomeEnabled→tracks-progress, dropPlayers→players-add-drop, advancedActions→campaign-advanced, guideposts→campaign-guideposts, minions→campaign-minions, campaignEnd→campaign-end). The existing Task-38 union/mechanics tests stay green (additive).
- **Campaign progress screen (Task 55):** `src/campaignUI.js` (NEW) → `createCampaignScreen(campaign, {container, onStartGame})` renders a `.gc-map` of 12 game nodes (done/current/future states from gameNumber, 🏆 win markers from gameResults, ▶ current marker), an "Unlocked Components" chip panel (constructed buildings, sticker names via `STICKER_DEFS`, crate numbers, story titles via `STORY_CARDS`, active rule flags), and a "Setup Checklist — Game N" list (`data-setup` items buildings/stickers/crates/archive/stories/players/rules) with a `data-action=start-game` Start button that fires `onStartGame(gameNumber)`. Reuses the `.g-game` container-query class + new `.gc-*` CSS in index.html.
- **In-app Chronicle (Task 56):** `createChronicleBrowser(chronicle, {container})` renders a search input (`#chronicleSearch`), an active-rule-flags chip row (per active flag: `STICKER_DEFS` name + `data-entries` linked ids), and the sections/entries filtered live by `chronicle.search(q)` (title + mechanics chips + text from `chronicle.entry(id)`).
- **Add/drop players (Task 57):** `src/campaignState.js` → `addPlayerToCampaign(campaign, {id, charterId, color, rng, cardPool})` validates (in-progress campaign, unique id, free charter 0-5), grants the charter color, equitable glory/capacity = floor(average of the others), and 1 random constructed/unconstructed building card (from `DEFAULT_CARDS`, excluding archived) recorded as `grantedCard`; `dropPlayerFromCampaign` frees the charter; `applyGrantedCards(state, campaign)` puts each `grantedCard` into that game's hand (idempotent, skips archived/missing). Player records now carry `charterId` (round-trips through campaignStateToJSON/FromJSON).
- **End-of-campaign scoring (Task 58):** `scoreCampaign(campaign, {buildingTiles})` requires `campaignComplete`; per player scores capacity (3 VP/space), used personas (7 VP each), victories (8 VP/win), glory (10 VP to the most-glory player, ties share), and building value (sum of printed tile VP of `constructedBuildings` by owner). Standings sort total→glory→building value→id; `winnerIds` = rank-1. `CAMPAIGN_SCORE_VALUES` fixes the printed table's range tops (capacityPerSpace 3, personaVp 7, victoryVp 8, gloryLeaderVp 10) — PROVISIONAL per the Icon Guide ranges.
- **Automa bot core (Task 59):** `src/automa.js` → `createSoloGame(config)` builds a 2-player state (human P1 charter 0 + `AUTOMA_ID` charter 1 with workers 0, coins 4, `progress`/income passthrough) with `g.automaData` (cubes 12, cubesUsed, placements, constructedCards, scoredObjectives, scoreEvents, vpMultiplier from `difficulty.multiplier`); `execAutomaBehavior(state, behavior, ctx)` executes any behavior descriptor (setup/place/retrieve/construct/crate/quota/scoreObjective/reputation/income/progress/difficulty/note) against the real subsystems — placement picks the first building free of a HUMAN worker (`pickAutomaTarget`, never bumps), construction uses `legalConstructionCellsForPlayer`, crates find an unlocked `cbldg-*` via `constructedCardWithCrate`, quota gains then sells on the open space, objectives use `engine.scoreObjective` on completed unscored revealed ids; every failure becomes a log note (never throws). `runAutomaTurn(state, ctx)` resolves the deck card `(seed + turnsTaken()) % len`, clamps phantom workers, executes, then advances the turn machine with `takeTurn(PLACE)`. The m59 test plays a full seeded solo game on a short custom progress track (`createProgressTrack({spaces:[null,null,null,null,null,"end"], startSpace:2})`) — scripted human alternates construction/treasury/retrieve — and asserts it completes without rule violations with end-game scoring for both players, Automa VP > 0, cubes placed, and multiple deck-driven turns.
- Tests: `src/campaignState.test.js` (Tasks 53-58) → `runCampaignStateTests()` (`?test=campaignstate`, 99 tests); `src/automa.test.js` (Tasks 44+59) → `runAutomaTests()` (`?test=automa`, 27 tests); `src/campaignUI.test.js` (NEW, Tasks 55-56) → `runCampaignUITests()` (`?test=campaignui`, 25 tests). All three registered in `__runAllTests` (26 suites). Vision checks: `scratch/shots/campaign-screen.png` and `scratch/shots/chronicle.png` (html-to-image captures, DOM assertions are authoritative).

### Appendix — Tasks 60-64 Phase 12 finish + Phase 13 AI opponents (reference for all phases)
- **Automa interaction & determinism (Task 60):** `src/automa.js` — `runAutomaTurn` indexes the Automa deck by the Automa's OWN turn count (`state.turns.counts()[automaId]`, per `automa-deck-cycling`), not the global turns-taken counter — with a human going first the old indexing sampled every other card, so the Automa never drew its construction/objective cards and games stalled short of the end. `execAutomaBehavior` gains: no-bump protection on named buildings (a `no-bump` log note when the human occupies the card's named building; the Automa still targets another building), `bump` behavior → "no-bump-per-rules" note, and a `misses` gate (`turnsTaken() % 4 === 0`) on construct/crate/quota/scoreObjective/reputation for easy difficulty. `runAutomaGame(config)` plays a full solo game: `createSoloGame` + alternating `defaultHumanPolicy`/`runAutomaTurn`, balancing the round, then `engine.endGame()` — returns `{g, ok, errors, steps, standings, winnerIds}`. `defaultHumanPolicy(g, ctx)` is a deterministic policy: reputation (if influence ≥ 5, keeps 4 in reserve) → construct a unique building (gathering its printed construction cost from the general supply first — the "gather then build" simplification) → score a completed unscored objective at the Grandstand → Treasury fallback → retrieve. The m60 tests assert byte-identical states (log `at` timestamps stripped) across identical seeds, identical construction multisets, adjacency of Automa-built buildings to its charter/own buildings, no-bump behavior, and full `runAutomaGame` determinism.
- **Automa difficulty (Task 61):** `AUTOMA_DIFFICULTIES` = easy `{multiplier:0.5, startCubes:10, misses:true, bonusConstruct:false}`, normal `{multiplier:1, startCubes:12}`, hard `{multiplier:1.5, startCubes:14, bonusConstruct:true}`; `normalizeDifficulty(d)` accepts strings or config objects. `createSoloGame` stores `automaData.automaId` and PATCHES the Automa player's `addVp` to scale every VP gain (construct/crate/objective/quota) by `vpMultiplier` — the engine grants objective/quota VP directly via `player.addVp`, so the patch is the single multiplier choke-point (and removed the previous double-count in `execAutomaBehavior`'s scoreObjective). `src/scoring.js` `scoreEndGame` now applies the multiplier to the Automa's reputation/objective/building/crate VP (all its scored sources, per automa-difficulty; reputation no longer stays unscaled). The m61 test samples 40 seeds × 3 difficulties on an 8-space track and asserts human win rate rises monotonically hard→easy (measured 100%/8%/0%) with all 120 games completing. Values are PROVISIONAL tuning constants — tune them against the same test if balance drifts.
- **AI decision core (Task 62):** `src/ai.js` (NEW) → `AI_DIFFICULTIES` (easy `{noise:0.7}` noisy random ~70% of the time; normal one-ply; hard `{lookahead:6}` two-ply), `normalizeAIDifficulty`, `RESOURCE_VALUES`/`itemValue`, `candidateActions(state, playerId)` enumerates EVERY legal move through `engine.checkPlace` (so candidates are legal by construction; per-building `opts`: treasury resource type, market mat card, grandstand objective, zeppelin card+cell, charterstone card, cloudport quota space), `scoreCandidate` (printed VP + benefit item value + owner-benefit ×0.3 + per-building bonuses: grandstand +5, zeppelin +5, charterstone +8, cloudport +3, market +3 for unconstructed building cards + zeppelin tile VP + cluster constructionScore), `proposeMove` (guards on-turn + non-empty legal actions; noise for easy; else `pickBestMove`), `evaluateTwoPly` (clone via `restoreGameState(JSON.parse(state.serialize()))`, apply candidate, let the opponent reply one-ply normal, position value = own VP+assets − opponent's), and `applyMove`. The m62 test plays ~1000 positions across easy/normal/hard games, randomizing resources/influence mid-game, asserting every proposed and every candidate move passes `checkPlace` with zero errors.
- **AI construction strategy (Task 63):** `aiConstructionCell(state, playerId, cardId)` ranks the player's legal construction cells by cluster adjacency (2/neighbour to own buildings), adjacency to the player's own charter (+0.5), printed tile VP (×0.5), and open expansion destinations (+0.25). `constructionScore` mirrors it (weighted 1.5/0.5/0.25) and resolves key-vs-cell inputs. The m63 test asserts every AI-chosen zeppelin construction cell is in `legalConstructionCellsForPlayer`, `aiConstructionCell` returns a legal cell for a held card, returns `no_legal_cells` when the board is full, and prefers the cell adjacent to the player's first building over isolated charter cells.
- **AI difficulty (Task 64):** `simulateAIGame(config)` runs a 2-player AI-vs-AI match (personas from STARTING_SETUP, optional `startingResources`/`startCards` — granted start cards should be REMOVED from the advancement deck to avoid duplicate building cards, which previously crashed construction's leftover gain; `engine.placeWorker` apply is now defensively guarded to skip an already-archived leftover). Returns `{g, steps, errors, ok, standings, winnerIds}`. The m64 test: 16 seeds alternating first player, hard (A) vs easy (B), and asserts hard wins a majority (measured 16/24 → after tuning easy noise 0.7 + hard lookahead 6, roughly 67%). `__runAllTests` is now 27 suites; `?test=ai` / `window.__loadAITests()`.
- **Note on the earlier freeze:** the initial `runAutomaGame` win-rate sweep (120 games × long track, single eval) exceeded the eval timeout while a mid-batch bug had the Automa sampling half its deck — the page's main thread stayed busy and the tab froze. Recovery was a reload; the fixes above (Automa deck indexing, human policy gathering construction resources, unique-building guard, shorter tracks, bounded evals) keep every game fast (~5-30ms) and bounded. Always verify a single small game before scaling a sweep.
### Appendix — Tasks 65-69 Phase 14 Multiplayer + Task 69 onboarding (reference for all phases)
- **Server-plugin room server (Tasks 65-68):** the FIRST `<script type="text/x-server-plugin">` element in index.html is an authoritative table server adapted from the BGN blank template (scratch/template-backup/index.html) to Charterstone: rooms hold 1-6 seats (seat index = charter + turn order), 5-letter join codes (`CODE_CHARS`), durable per-room slots in the plugin `state` bytes (SLOT ≤ 64 KiB, HEADER 26, up to 6×26 bytes of seat names, SNAP_MAX ≈ 48 KiB for the full serialized game, CAMPAIGN_MAX 16 KiB; MAX_ROOMS computed from state.length). RPCs: `createRoom` (host → seat 0), `joinRoom` (by preferred seat or first open; idempotent for a conn already in the room; returns code+seat+snap), `getRoom`, `startGame` (host only, waiting→playing), `move` (validates bound seat, monotonic turn counter — stale/bad/rate-limited rejected, stores the full snapshot, bumps turn, rebroadcasts), `endGame`/resign, `submitCampaign` (stores the campaign JSON string), `chat` (700 ms/conn + 2.5 s/net-group, 160 chars, banned words), `leave`, `online` (global conn count). Presence rides on every join/leave/onclose via a lightweight `t`:"presence"` broadcast; the global N-players-online publishes on topic `st:on`. Leavers do NOT forfeit (unlike BGN) so a room survives until all seats reconnect; abandoned rooms are reclaimed oldest-first by `findSlot`. Server code is PUBLIC source — no secrets ever. The server is intentionally free of game logic: the snapshot's own whoseTurn is the game authority, the server only guards ordering/integrity.
- **Online client (Tasks 65-68):** `src/online.js` → `createOnlineClient({name, socketFactory, autoReconnect})` wraps the WebSocket-facade socket: `connect()`/opened, `createRoom`, `joinRoom` (persists code+seat client-side so a reconnect re-binds the same seat), `getRoom`, `startGame`, `submitMove({turn,snapshot})`, `endGame({winner})` (seat index or "draw"), `resign`, `submitCampaign`, `chat`, `leave`, `online()`, `rawRpc`, and an event emitter (on/off for open/close/snap/presence/chat/online). Reconnect is capped-backoff (×1.7 to 15 s, max 8 tries) and gives up on close codes 4403/1000; on re-open it re-joins its room and re-syncs the snapshot. Known fix: `close()` must remove listeners BEFORE closing the underlying socket (it previously nulled the socket first, so disconnects never reached the server).
- **Online campaign persistence (Task 67):** the campaign JSON (from `campaignStateToJSON`) + the latest game snapshot ride the room's durable slot, so a room closed after game 3 reopens at game 4 with every legacy sticker/building intact — verified end-to-end in `src/online.test.js`.
- **Chat & presence (Task 68):** table chat broadcasts to every subscribed seat with the sender's seat/name; presence events carry the live seat map + online count; the global online count is shared across rooms.
- **Tutorial (Task 69):** `src/tutorial.js` → `createTutorialGame({seed})` (deterministic single-player Game-1 state: P1 on turn, 4 coins, Mine card + its 4 construction resources + 1 clay primed so every action is legal) and `createTutorial(state, {container})` — 5 steps (setup → firstPlace at the Treasury → retrieve → construct the Mine at the Zeppelin on a legal charter-adjacent cell → done), each step's `action` drives the REAL engine API (`engine.checkPlace`, `engine.placeWorker`, `engine.retrieveWorkers`, `legalConstructionCellsForPlayer`, `board.constructedBuildings`) and must return ok. The overlay UI (injectTutorialStyles + render) shows title/body/hint, a Do-it button that runs the step's action and reports success/failure, Back/Next/Finish, and progress dots; the hero's "Learn to Play" button launches it (`window.__activeTutorial`).
- Tests: `src/online.test.js` (NEW, Tasks 65-68) → `runOnlineTests()` (`?test=online`, async, 42 tests) and `src/tutorial.test.js` (NEW, Task 69) → `runTutorialTests()` (`?test=tutorial`, async, 19 tests). `__runAllTests` is now 29 suites. These run in the unsaved-editor EMULATOR (same-document sockets share the emulated server, state is ephemeral until the user saves) — real cross-tab multiplayer requires the generator to be saved/published.

### Appendix — Tasks 70-79 Phase 15-17 onboarding, audio, accessibility, perf & post-game (reference for all phases)
- **How-to-play guide (Task 70):** `src/howToPlay.js` → `HOW_TO_PLAY_SECTIONS` (4 sections: Turn Structure, The Commons, The Tracks, The Legacy Flow), each with `chronicleEntryIds` linking to its ACTIVE Chronicle entry (`turns-actions`…`campaign-end`); `createHowToPlayGuide({chronicle})` resolves them via `chronicle.entry(id)`; `createHowToPlayModal` renders tabbed sections + "In the Chronicle" entry cards. Test: every section links to a real entry in the in-app Chronicle. Wired to the hero's "How to Play" button.
- **Icon legend (Task 71):** `src/iconLegend.js` → `ICON_LEGEND` covers all 7 resources (incl. coins, which is currency, not a RESOURCE_TYPES entry) + influence, VP, quota, reputation, income, crate, objective, campaign, worker, bump, progress; `legendFor`/`tooltipFor`/alias legend ids; `renderLegendChips` + `createIconLegendModal`. Every chip's tooltip round-trips to its entry (the test's contract). Wired to the hero's "Icon Legend" button.
- **Save slots & continue (Task 72):** `src/saves.js` → `createSaves({kv})` over kv folder `charterstone-saves`: `list/save/load/remove/autosave/latest/continueCampaign`. A slot stores `state.toJSON()` and/or `campaignStateToJSON`; `continueCampaign` prefers the autosave (mid-game board, round-trips byte-identically) and otherwise resumes an between-games campaign record (gameNumber intact → beginNextGame). Test drives both paths against the real serializers.
- **Sound effects (Task 73):** `src/sfx.js` → `createSFX()` synthesizes place/bump/coin/construct/crate/end with WebAudio oscillators (no assets); `setMuted` is the mute contract — while muted ZERO oscillators are scheduled; `unlock()` must be called from a user gesture. Test asserts the scheduled-count contract.
- **Ambient music (Task 74):** `src/music.js` → two uploaded same-family tracks (village theme + market variant; MP3s generated this session and uploaded — URLs in `MUSIC_TRACKS`, rebuild recipe in the file comment) with a gesture-gated `unlock()`/start and gapless looping via a single `<audio>` per region (loop=true, no re-trigger seams); `setRegion` crossfades between regions. Test: no start before gesture, loops with loop=true, region switching works.
- **End-of-game polish (Task 75):** `src/gameEnd.js` → `scoreBreakdown` mirrors `scoreEndGame`'s four sources (reputation/objective/building/crate); `createEndGameModal` crowns the winner(s) with an animated banner (reduced-motion aware) and a per-player source-by-source VP table. Test: the modal lists each scoring source's VP exactly as scored. Wired into the live game (startGameDemo): after the final action the modal appears; every act also plays the matching SFX.
- **Accessibility (Task 76):** `src/accessibility.js` → keyboard-only action flow (`createAccessibility({state, ui, container})`: P=place, R=retrieve, arrows/WASD move the focus cursor, Enter/Space select (and Enter confirms once a selection is armed), 1-9 pick options, C=confirm, Esc=cancel); `PLAYER_SHAPES`/shapeForCharter make tokens color-blind-safe (worker dots in gameUI now carry their charter's shape glyph with an AA-computed text color); `prefersReducedMotion` adds a `cs-reduced-motion` class; WCAG-AA helpers (`luminance`/`contrastRatio`/`isAAOnDark`/`accessibleTextColor`) — the sixth charter color was lightened `#8e5fbf → #9b6cc9` so ALL six pass AA (4.2→5.0) on the dark board. Test drives the whole turn flow with synthetic key events. Wired into the live game.
- **Performance safeguards (Task 77):** `src/perf.js` → `measureFrame`, `batchedRender` (single string build), `createDirtyFlags`, `memoize` (LRU), `cullCells`, `renderStressBoard(n)`, and `gameRenderFingerprint`. gameUI now: skips re-render when the fingerprint + interaction state is unchanged (dirty flag), memoizes `describeBuilding` per state (memoized card data), and culls off-viewport hexes on boards larger than 120 cells. Test: a 200-building stress board renders under 16ms per frame.
- **Campaign-complete mode (Task 78):** `src/campaignState.js` → `createReplayGame(campaign, baseConfig)`: when `campaignComplete`, builds a fresh NON-legacy worker-placement config on the FINAL board (setupNextGame over the campaign's legacy — buildings, stickers, crates, archive) with `gameNumber = min(gameNumber, 12)`, `replay: true`, and no campaign linkage (replaying never advances the campaign; it can be replayed repeatedly from the same final village). Test: 12 finished games → replay on the final board, beginNextGame refuses, replay turns resolve, campaign untouched.
- **Campaign summary (Task 79):** `src/campaignSummary.js` → `buildCampaignSummary` derives per-player wins/glory/capacity/personas/buildings(+printed tile VP)/crates strictly from campaign history; `campaignSummaryText` (copyable) + `createCampaignSummaryScreen` modal with per-player rows, totals, Copy + Export-JSON buttons. Test: every summary field matches the campaign state history.
- Tests (NEW): `howToPlay.test.js` (18), `iconLegend.test.js` (29), `saves.test.js` (13), `sfx.test.js` (10), `music.test.js` (14), `gameEnd.test.js` (11), `accessibility.test.js` (22), `perf.test.js` (15), `campaignComplete.test.js` (25), `campaignSummary.test.js` (34). `__runAllTests` is now 39 suites (1005 tests). Known guard: the a11y keyboard Enter-confirm contract needed gameUI `chooseOption` to accept numeric indices and `ui.getSel()/getMode()` getters; gameUI render now short-circuits on an unchanged fingerprint — all prior suites still pass.
### Appendix — Task 80 publish metadata & discovery polish (reference for all phases)
- **Discovery metadata (Task 80):** the `$meta` block in main.pjs is the platform source of truth for the generator listing page and social share cards — `title` ("Charterstone — Build Your Legacy"), a ~300-char `description` (names the 12-game legacy campaign, 1-6 players, online play, solo Automa), 10 lowercase `tags`, and `image` = the branded hero (uploaded `https://user.uploads.dev/file/95b42b398dedb399141e07015172aea4.jpg`, verified live JPEG). `src/metadata.js` mirrors those facts as `DISCOVERY` (title/description/tags/image) + `META_VERSION` so they're verifiable at runtime — keep the two in sync.
- **Branded hero + copy:** the landing page's hero renders the same image (`heroBgImg.src === root.heroImage`) over the kicker/title/tagline/genre/players, three campaign cards, four founding facts, and the footer credit; the listing screenshot (platform capture of this page) therefore shows the branded hero + copy, and share cards use the same hero via `$meta.image`. `buildStatus` now reads "complete — all 80 roadmap tasks across 17 phases" (the statusbar), and the roadmap modal counts 80/80 ticked.
- Test: `src/metadata.test.js` (NEW) → `runMetadataTests()` (`?test=metadata`, async, 25 tests) — asserts the DISCOVERY mirror (title/description sizing, lowercase tags, hosted https hero URL), the hero image actually loading, the landing hero + all brand copy rendering (title/kicker/tagline/genre/players/facts/cards/footer/statusbar), `$meta.header.mode === "minimal"`, and a fetched `src/roadmap.pjs` with exactly 80 tasks all ticked. Registered in `__runAllTests` → 40 suites (1030 tests). The actual platform listing/share-card render can't be observed from inside the iframe, so the suite validates the facts that feed it (live branded hero + full landing copy) — the editor's own preview mirrors what the listing screenshot shows.
